---
import { docs } from "../data/site";

// Four things every bundle carries; samples are the one optional addition.
const parts = [
  {
    title: "The model, in its own format",
    text: "Each library keeps its native serialization. The model is stored as it is, never converted and never pickled.",
  },
  {
    title: "A manifest of what it claims",
    text: "Class, hyperparameters, feature names and order, class labels, the Python it was trained with, and a SHA-256 hash for each of the other files.",
  },
  {
    title: "A schema made on your machine",
    text: "MLschema describes each input from the training DataFrame locally. The DataFrame itself is not written to the bundle.",
  },
  {
    title: "A lock of what serving needs",
    text: "A pylock.toml with the exact Python and the Linux and Windows wheels for the versions you trained with. MLbundle records it and never installs it; your server decides what to trust.",
  },
];
---

<section class="section inside" id="inside" aria-labelledby="inside-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="section-head">
      <h2 id="inside-title">One file, four parts.</h2>
      <p>
        A <code class="mono">.mlbundle</code> is a ZIP with a closed list of files. Anything else
        inside it is rejected before the model is read.
      </p>
    </header>
    <ul class="parts">
      {parts.map(({ title, text }) => (
        <li>
          <h3>{title}</h3>
          <p>{text}</p>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <p class="aside">
      Models with readable internals, such as trees and linear coefficients, also carry that
      structure for review, checked against the loaded model. Training rows are added only when you
      ask for them with <code class="mono">sample_rows=N</code>.
      <a class="text-link" href={docs.format}>
        Format specification <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
      </a>
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
